it's the last day of the Harvest in  svalov in southern Sweden  but this wheat isn't destined for the  nation's bread baskets this is the test  field at the lant Menon farming  Cooperative where manager Tina Henriksen  has a mission  to breed crops that are future proof we  develop new varieties of wheat to make  sure that the varieties can still  produce as big yields as possible even  if the climate changes what we really  want to produce is varieties that are  robust to a lot of different climates  for her team it's been a decade-long  process  45 000 types of wheat have been whittled  down to an elite chief of just 10.  resistant to extreme heat drought and  floods  the knowledge could be crucial further  south the worst droughts in 500 years  have caused crops to fail in Italy  France and Germany  farming here used to be challenging  because of the cold not anymore  2022 has been a bumper Harvest for  Sweden an indication perhaps that their  agricultural industry can benefit at  least in the short term from the effects  of climate change  as the heat moves North Sweden's growing  season has become longer and warmer  septembers in the South have been two  degrees Celsius above the average in  recent years  farmers are aware that the good times  might not last long this Farm has been  record-breaking really we've had all the  all the great components related to a  good year we will probably see more  extreme weathers it seems like in this  region that once the rain comes it comes  more and once the dry weather comes it's  it's for longer periods changes in  climate bring changes in disease death  causes a toxin something that the  breeding process also tries to combat  Sweden's harvests may help with the  current food shortages in other  countries  but in the long term the biggest yields  may be in the lab fulleries Al Jazeera  sparlov Sweden
 

